  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component fails core accessibility requirements for form inputs. It lacks a programmatic label, does not associate error messages with the input, and fails to communicate state changes (invalid/error) to assistive technology. While keyboard navigation works, screen reader users are left without context for the field or its validation state.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1. Missing `<label>` element associated with the file input.\n2. Error message rendered visually but not programmatically linked via `aria-describedby`.\n3. `aria-invalid` state missing on the input during error conditions.\n4. No dynamic announcement (`aria-live`) for the validation error.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Missing Accessible Name for File Input**\n   - Evidence: `<div>Upload file</div>` is visually present but not associated with `<input type=\"file\">`. Screen readers will announce the native UA string \"Choose File\" instead of the intended label. Confirmed by axe-core `label` rule firing (critical impact) against the input selector.\n   - User Group: Screen reader users.\n   - WCAG/APG: 1.3.1 Info and Relationships, 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - Fix: Replace `<div>Upload file</div>` with `<label htmlFor=\"file-input\">Upload file</label>` and add `id=\"file-input\"` to the input.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Error Message Not Associated with Input**\n   - Evidence: `{error && <div className=\"error-message\">{error}</div>}` renders visually but lacks an `id` and is not referenced by the input via `aria-describedby`. The screen-reader census shows \"Upload file\" and \"File too large\" as separate, unlinked spoken phrases.\n   - User Group: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users.\n   - WCAG/APG: 3.3.1 Error Identification, 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - Fix: Add `id=\"file-error\"` to the error div and `aria-describedby=\"file-error\"` to the input.\n\n2. **Invalid State Not Communicated Programmatically**\n   - Evidence: `setError('File too large')` updates UI but does not update `aria-invalid={!!error}` on the input. The trace shows `invalid: \"false\"` even when an error state exists in the component logic.\n   - User Group: Screen reader users.\n   - WCAG/APG: 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - Fix: Add `aria-invalid={!!error}` to the `<input>`.\n\n3. **Dynamic Error Not Announced**\n   - Evidence: Error message is a conditional `<div>` without `aria-live`. Screen readers won't announce it when it appears unless focus moves there. The trace confirms `live_announcements` remains empty across steps.\n   - User Group: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users.\n   - WCAG/APG: 4.1.3 Status Messages.\n   - Fix: Add `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live=\"assertive\"` to the error div.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Missing Page Structure Context**: The evidence digest shows axe-core flagged missing landmarks (`landmark-one-main`, `region`) and headings (`page-has-heading-one`). While these are page-level issues, they impact the component's context. Ensure the parent layout provides `<main>`, `<h1>`, etc.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- **Non-Color Error Indicators**: Verify that the error message uses a non-color indicator (e.g., an icon or text prefix like \"Error:\") in addition to color, per WCAG 1.4.1.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- Programmatic label association (`htmlFor`/`id`).\n- Error-to-input association (`aria-describedby`).\n- Invalid state reflection (`aria-invalid`).\n- Live region for dynamic error announcement (`aria-live=\"assertive\"`).\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Hears \"Choose File\" (native) instead of \"Upload file\". When an error occurs, the message appears visually but is not announced. If they tab to the input, they might hear the error if it's in the reading order, but without `aria-describedby`, the relationship isn't explicit.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Tab/Shift+Tab works correctly. Focus moves to the input. No traps.\n- **Low vision user**: Visual error is present. If color is the only indicator (e.g., red text), it fails WCAG 1.4.1. Ensure non-color indicators are used.\n- **Cognitive accessibility user**: Error message appears visually but might be missed if not announced or clearly linked to the field.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component has critical gaps in form labeling and error communication that block screen reader users from understanding the field's purpose and validation state. These are fundamental WCAG 2.2 AA failures. The design needs revision to establish programmatic associations and dynamic state announcements.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the parent layout provide necessary landmarks and headings? (Addressed in Minor Findings)\n- Is color used as the sole indicator for the error message? (Needs verification against CSS)",
